// The other direction: a bare `Ctor` claims the case has no payload, and stays
// an error when it does. The message names the `(..)` form, since "I don't care
// what it holds" is the usual reason someone reaches for the bare name here.
variant V = A(i64) | B

fn f(v: V) -> i64 {
    match (v) {
        A => 1,
        B => 2,
    }
}

fn main() -> i64 { f(B) }
--- errors ---
error: constructor "A@V" binds 1 value(s), but 0 given (write "A(..)" to match it without naming the payload)
 --> ./tests/cases/match_arms/err_nullary_for_payload.aipl:8:9
  |
8 |         A => 1,
  |         ^
